A provider says item IDs and decimal revision strings are scoped to (session,item); revisions compare as arbitrary-size non-negative integers and may exceed JavaScript's safe-integer range. Within each such pair, input updates are in increasing revision order. Consider:

type U = { session: string; item: string; revision: string; state: 'open' | 'done' };
function doneFor(session: string, updates: U[]): string[] {
  const latest = new Map<string,U>();
  for (const u of updates) {
    const old = latest.get(u.item);
    if (!old || u.revision > old.revision) latest.set(u.item,u);
  }
  return [...latest.values()].filter(u => u.session === session && u.state === 'done').map(u => u.item).sort();
}

Input, in order: S1/x rev '9' open; S2/x rev '99' done; S1/x rev '10' done; S1/y rev '1' done. For doneFor('S1', input), state the current result, the required result, and the smallest robust repair. Address every independent defect; converting revisions to Number is not allowed.
